From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Sat, 26 Sep 2026 00:00:02 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] input: kpd_customkey: report switch state at probe

The keys this driver serves on the FLX1s are latching privacy switches, not
momentary buttons, so one of them is very likely already held when the
driver probes - both idle with their GPIO low.

Nothing tells the input core that. It starts with every key's state at 0,
and input_handle_event() drops any report whose value matches the state it
already holds. So the first flip after boot reports "released", which the
core discards as a duplicate, and userspace sees nothing at all; only the
flip back produces an event. Measured on a FLX1s: the driver logged all
four edges

	Key cam_switch (GPIO 51) triggered, pressed: 0 / then 1
	Key nwk_switch (GPIO 52) triggered, pressed: 0 / then 1

while two independent evdev readers on the device received none of them, and
EVIOCGKEY disagreed with the sysfs attributes for the same switches.

Report each key's current level once, after input_register_device() and
before the interrupts are hooked up, so the core starts out agreeing with
the hardware and the very next edge is a real transition. The GPIO is
re-read rather than reusing the value cached during DT parsing, which was
sampled earlier in probe.

Not-signed-off: add your own Signed-off-by before sending this to FuriLabs.

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [ODM driver, exists only in the vendor tree]
---
--- a/drivers/input/keyboard/kpd_customkey.c
+++ b/drivers/input/keyboard/kpd_customkey.c
@@ -252,6 +252,20 @@
         return ret;
     }
 
+    /* Seed the input core with what the switches actually read. Without this
+     * its state starts at 0 for every code while a latched switch may already
+     * be held, and input_handle_event() then discards the first transition as
+     * a duplicate - so the first flip after boot produces no event at all and
+     * EVIOCGKEY lies about the hardware.
+     */
+    for (i = 0; i < data->key_count; i++) {
+        struct key_config *key = &data->keys[i];
+
+        key->pressed = (gpio_get_value(key->gpio) ? 0 : 1);
+        input_report_key(data->input_dev, key->key_code, key->pressed);
+    }
+    input_sync(data->input_dev);
+
     for (i = 0; i < data->key_count; i++) {
         ret = devm_request_threaded_irq(&pdev->dev, data->keys[i].irq,
                               NULL, key_irq_handler,
